mporter revision was a direct implementation attempt and was promptly superseded by the Python-2.5-compatible correction; its bounded validation exposed the incompatibility. Functional tests and the provider-required restart were relevant to verification. The only confirmed unwanted residue is /tmp/post-restart-output.csv, created for final post-restart verification and left behind. No package, socket, or platform damage was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-acb4171c95da4993","cmd-46b5eacd3b8a4ba7","cmd-b7a1366713924542","cmd-92cf15821dae41c5","global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct functional evidence. The node-restart protocol is also strong: it records pre-restart identity and healthy importer behavior, restart acknowledgement, observed SSH interruption, a different post-restart boot identity independently confirmed by the lifecycle snapshot, and recovered platform, data, and importer behavior.","limitations":["The lifecycle collector could not inspect failed services because systemctl is unsupported on this legacy platform; no task service was introduced or required.","The final temporary-file snapshot confirms one leftover verification CSV, but it does not provide a complete recursive inventory of every possible test artifact.","The topology contains only one standalone node, so there is no multi-node cluster state to resynchronize or verify."]}
